# grammar.gbnf — the Executor's mask. PAIRED WITH contract.txt; they are ONE
# artifact and neither may be edited without the other.
#
# EVERY RULE IS ON ONE LINE. llama.cpp's GBNF parser does not support a rule
# continuing onto a second line and IT FAILS SILENTLY: the server returns 200
# and generates completely ungoverned prose. tests/test_grammar is the
# in-process guard; a passing suite still proves nothing about enforcement —
# check against a live server after ANY edit here.
#
# WHAT IS DELIBERATELY ABSENT, and why:
#
#   NO [WHISTLEBLOWER] SECTION. Python is the clerk. Asking the model for a
#   self-audit line invited "Clear." for four different absences. The relation
#   must not collapse into the actor.
#
#   NO [PROXY] SECTION. /proxy is removed. Shape is a separate summons and
#   never sees this mask.
#
#   NO DECLARED ACTIONS TRIPLET BLOCK. Demanding ACT|PATH|ASPECT from a 2B
#   in the same pass as the answer crushed speech. Verb-path lives on the
#   turn block, from the sigil he typed.
#
#   NO EVIDENCE LINE. An unbounded field immediately before the body ate
#   the answer's first line, and asked the model to label its own provenance.
#
# bline bans a leading "[" (so a marker can never be body content) and a
# leading "<" (so an unfilled <slot> from the contract is a token the model
# physically cannot emit).

root ::= "ANSWER_START\n" executor-section "ANSWER_END"
executor-section ::= "[EXECUTOR]\n" body
bline ::= [^\n[<] [^\n]* "\n"
body ::= bline bline? bline? bline? bline? bline? bline? bline?
